ional consequences without severing their evidence roots."}, "working_set_metrics": {"delivered_context_chars": 27431, "inquiry_drive_project_count": 3, "mode": "bounded", "retrieval_candidate_count": 12, "retrieval_evidence_count": 10, "retrieval_rehydrated_evidence_count": 2, "retrieval_trigger_counts": {"notebook_revised": 3, "project_source_handoff": 3, "unincorporated_evidence": 6}, "trust_compact_candidate_count": 1, "trust_compact_evidence_root_count": 7, "trust_compact_settled_count": 1, "working_set_chars": 4191, "working_to_delivered_ratio": 0.1528}, "working_set_shadow": {"active_projects": [{"id": "proj-assumption-falsification", "next_step": "Integrate failure attribution metrics from DeFA (source-b3c73db2a88d4644) into the counterfactual scoring framework to refine error identification.", "question": "Can automated reasoning systems reliably detect and correct false foundational premises without external oversight?", "title": "Challenging Foundational Assumptions in Automated Reasoning"}, {"id": "proj-autonomous-safety-analysis", "next_step": "Benchmark current CRASH adaptation performance against the failure-propagation graphs defined in recent DeFA studies.", "question": "How can cognitive reasoning agents designed for incident reporting, such as CRASH, be adapted to automate safety incident analysis across diverse, non-standardized industrial domains?", "title": "Autonomous Safety Analysis in Complex Systems"}, {"id": "proj-metacognitive-blind-spots", "next_step": "Implement a pilot anomaly detection module for agent trajectories that leverages the dependency graph construction identified in DeFA research.", "question": "How can autonomous agents identify reasoning blind spots that their architectural designers have not explicitly mitigated?", "title": "Detecting Unrecognized Reasoning Blind Spots in LLMs"}], "beliefs": [{"claim": "Automated reasoning models exhibit distinct safety failure profiles under adversarial pressure, preventing universal self-correction strategies; this is reinforced by the finding that specific error attribution is highly sensitive to cross-segment dependencies.", "confidence": 0.9, "falsifier": "Demonstration of a universal, context-independent error correction algorithm that successfully mitigates failures across heterogeneous tasks without introducing new failure modes.", "id": "belief-assumption-failure-modes", "provenance": ["source-754262e2b3954a03", "source-e9e2c11c527c4d6b", "source-ed95ef98c2374a5f", "source-856efae667f24740", "source-417b1103657e427f", "source-344ada5e7f2143a1", "source-b3c73db2a88d4644"], "status": "active", "why_retained": "New research on dependency-guided failure attribution (DeFA) confirms that reasoning errors are often trace-specific, limiting the efficacy of simple, universal correction mechanisms."}], "mode": "shadow", "open_commitments": [], "principle": "Keep exact receipts externally; carry the smallest useful abstraction that stays cheap to correct.", "recent_notebooks": [{"id": "notebook-assumption-falsification", "project": "proj-assumption-falsification", "provenance": ["source-7af05705615d4bcb", "source-eec50f13d0c74d14"], "revision": 10, "summary": "Automated reasoning systems struggle with foundational premise validation. Recent integration of causal-guided knowledge editing (CAKE) and failure attribution metrics suggests that explicitly modeling causal dependencies can help identify and rectify foundational errors that adversarial prompts exploit.", "title": "Analysis of Counterfactual Scoring and Foundational Reasoning Limits"}, {"id": "notebook-crash-industrial-adaptation", "project": "proj-autonomous-safety-analysis", "provenance": ["source-7af05705615d4bcb", "source-ec83d51858f24f47"], "revision": 4, "summary": "Adapting the CRASH agent framework to diverse industrial domains requires robust failure attribution. By applying causal inference methods to incident reports, agents can map failures back to decisive error steps, improving s